1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:13,551
How this modern marvel came to be and what it represents are intimately linked to how an industrious people tried to establish a sense of order in the midst of

2
00:00:13,552 --> 00:00:15,000
continual change.

3
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:27,000
Its legend is a story of hopes and dreams, money and power, of climbing your way to heaven and looking back down to remember who you are.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,540
New York has been called the center of the known universe.

5
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:42,680
A city based on the notion of cooperative diversity.

6
00:00:43,440 --> 00:00:47,260
Fed by the dreams of two centuries of immigrant culture.

7
00:00:48,220 --> 00:00:53,100
As people passed through the portals of Ellis Island to take a chance on the American dream,

8
00:00:53,740 --> 00:00:56,140
New York became a cradle for a new life.

9
00:00:56,141 --> 00:01:01,400
As a result, the city's image is forever linked to the bittersweet dramas of families

10
00:01:01,401 --> 00:01:06,180
who took a brisk walk off the edge of the old world and into the new,

11
00:01:06,840 --> 00:01:09,840
a land of opportunity and adventure.

12
00:01:20,840 --> 00:01:23,340
Throughout the first half of the 20th century,

13
00:01:23,341 --> 00:01:26,580
the spirit was embodied in the person of Al Smith,

14
00:01:27,340 --> 00:01:30,420
a born politician who rose from local office

15
00:01:30,421 --> 00:01:33,000
to become governor of New York in 1918.

16
00:01:34,060 --> 00:01:37,540
He was a symbol of the little guy with more moxie than money.

17
00:01:38,260 --> 00:01:40,440
He came from the streets of the Lower East Side,

18
00:01:40,520 --> 00:01:43,220
and like the city itself, he was everybody's pal.

19
00:01:43,221 --> 00:01:47,380
Falling down, boys and girls together,

20
00:01:48,420 --> 00:01:51,060
me and Amy O'Rourke,

21
00:01:51,061 --> 00:01:59,520
We've set the light fantastic out of the sidewalks of New York.

22
00:02:02,060 --> 00:02:07,740
Al Smith could identify with the social challenges of early immigrant life.

23
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:15,480
When I was a boy, we had no playgrounds and very few parks.

24
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:18,100
But we had something else.

25
00:02:18,101 --> 00:02:22,440
in its time just as good.

26
00:02:23,440 --> 00:02:24,680
And that was the waterfront.

27
00:02:25,140 --> 00:02:26,620
Swimming in the summertime.

28
00:02:27,060 --> 00:02:28,440
No small little pool.

29
00:02:30,600 --> 00:02:34,560
Smith's personality promoted a sense of common accomplishment.

30
00:02:35,140 --> 00:02:37,360
That was the key to maintaining the vitality

31
00:02:37,361 --> 00:02:40,320
of the modern urban experience which New York pioneered.

32
00:02:41,320 --> 00:02:44,740
He was the primary link to the cacophony of culture

33
00:02:44,741 --> 00:02:46,840
that exploded during the jazz age

34
00:02:46,841 --> 00:02:54,380
when New York had its own aggressive sense of style, recreation, and celebration of music and art.

35
00:02:55,140 --> 00:03:00,980
And the scandalous dancing of Josephine Baker put the world on notice that anything goes.

36
00:03:02,060 --> 00:03:07,000
The city was constantly reinventing itself in bolder and broader forms.

37
00:03:07,640 --> 00:03:14,560
Throughout his career, Al Smith seemed to integrate these elements into a singular brash personality.

38
00:03:14,561 --> 00:03:22,120
He asked me what I thought about the New Deal, and I didn't know whether he'd catch it or not, but I told him I didn't think it was so hot.

39
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:33,706
Smith reflected the kind of audacious attitude that had earlier prompted the community to conceive and build such grand projects as the famous mass transit system, the

40
00:03:33,707 --> 00:03:40,940
Brooklyn Bridge, and the sprawling commercial districts where the heart of the city beats its most furious rhythms.

41
00:03:40,941 --> 00:03:49,940
eventually New York attracted too many dreamers over seven million by the turn of the century

42
00:03:49,941 --> 00:03:55,820
the city desperately needed room to grow it was the architects challenge to find a way

43
00:03:55,821 --> 00:03:58,460
and Al Smith was determined to make it happen

44
00:03:58,461 --> 00:04:10,260
as the 20th century exploded on the scene New York became the acknowledged business capital

45
00:04:10,261 --> 00:04:11,207
of the world.

46
00:04:11,208 --> 00:04:16,380
Thousands of new enterprises vied for position, but with only 22 square

47
00:04:16,381 --> 00:04:22,320
miles available on Manhattan, the city was soon bursting skyward from its bedrock foundations.

48
00:04:23,520 --> 00:04:27,180
Increased building height demanded stronger structural support.

49
00:04:27,181 --> 00:04:28,400
Brick and mortar were

50
00:04:28,401 --> 00:04:30,866
replaced by steel beams and rivets.

51
00:04:30,867 --> 00:04:34,460
By 1903, early high-rise projects like the Flatiron

52
00:04:34,461 --> 00:04:39,313
Building reached the dizzying height of 20 stories.

53
00:04:39,314 --> 00:04:43,120
The Flatiron Building was a very intense

54
00:04:43,121 --> 00:04:48,440
effort to build a skyscraper fitting a very singular site, and it was really thought by

55
00:04:48,441 --> 00:04:52,700
many people that the building would blow over in a high wind, although of course it was steel

56
00:04:52,701 --> 00:04:55,539
construction and wasn't going to blow over at all.

57
00:04:55,540 --> 00:04:57,980
But it was a thrilling building in its day,

58
00:04:57,981 --> 00:05:05,760
steel frame but clad in stone, very handsome and proving that the skyscraper was well worth developing.

59
00:05:06,500 --> 00:05:13,160
The new steel frame construction ensured that buildings like the Flatiron would not topple in stiff winds.

60
00:05:13,720 --> 00:05:18,980
Architects began to realize that 20th century design would be more functional than ornamental.

61
00:05:20,220 --> 00:05:26,680
There were other problems associated with big buildings, including integrated heating, lighting and water installations

62
00:05:26,681 --> 00:05:29,700
that could handle the demands of many occupants.

63
00:05:30,320 --> 00:05:34,040
Architects now had to design systems versus just aesthetics,

64
00:05:34,380 --> 00:05:37,800
and they had to resolve the most daunting dilemma of all.

65
00:05:39,020 --> 00:05:42,120
How would people walk up all those stairs?

66
00:05:43,640 --> 00:05:47,000
There's no way anything of any major significance

67
00:05:47,001 --> 00:05:49,500
could have been built in height without the elevator.

68
00:05:49,501 --> 00:05:53,760
It's kind of tough convincing people to walk up 100 stories.

69
00:05:53,761 --> 00:06:00,760
So the elevator is of more than casual consequence.

70
00:06:00,761 --> 00:06:05,760
The evolution of the elevator is a classic example of American ingenuity.

71
00:06:05,761 --> 00:06:12,760
In 1852, an obscure engineer named Elisha Otis invented a unique new hoisting machine.

72
00:06:12,761 --> 00:06:18,760
Originally designed for freight and animals, it was eventually adapted for lifting people.

73
00:06:18,761 --> 00:06:20,312
In May of 1854, P.T.

74
00:06:20,313 --> 00:06:29,000
Barnum himself invited Otis to the first World's Fair in New York's Crystal Palace to demonstrate his invention.

75
00:06:30,200 --> 00:06:36,220
As an astonished crowd watched in horror, Otis rode his safety hoister up and down,

76
00:06:36,640 --> 00:06:40,700
then dramatically suffered the rope that supported the box he was riding.

77
00:06:41,140 --> 00:06:45,920
But instead of falling to his death, the box came to a gentle stop.

78
00:06:45,921 --> 00:06:49,200
He had also invented a fail-safe braking device.

79
00:06:49,880 --> 00:06:54,000
The world took notice, and the Otis elevator was born.

80
00:06:55,680 --> 00:07:00,740
Meanwhile, architects realized that all the usual dangers that threaten a building's welfare

81
00:07:00,741 --> 00:07:05,200
are magnified in large buildings, particularly the threat of fire.

82
00:07:06,320 --> 00:07:09,260
New York's biggest buildings often housed sweatshops,

83
00:07:09,800 --> 00:07:12,740
dark, dirty secret factories of the garment trade.

84
00:07:12,741 --> 00:07:19,640
Here, women and children worked long hours for meager wages in a kind of industrial hell.

85
00:07:20,860 --> 00:07:25,840
In 1911, disaster struck when New York's Triangle Shirtwaist Factory caught fire.

86
00:07:26,580 --> 00:07:31,680
The firemen fought into the night to a chorus of screams from women dying inside.

87
00:07:32,520 --> 00:07:37,800
Of 148 victims, scores were suffocated or burned beyond recognition,

88
00:07:38,520 --> 00:07:42,940
Trapped in a poorly designed building with inadequate exits and narrow hallways,

89
00:07:43,500 --> 00:07:46,520
others in terror leapt to their death on the sidewalk below

90
00:07:46,521 --> 00:07:50,400
as teams of rescue workers stood helplessly nearby.

91
00:07:51,520 --> 00:07:55,140
Al Smith personally conducted the accident investigation

92
00:07:55,141 --> 00:08:01,220
and introduced legislation that would create safer zoning regulations for large structures.

93
00:08:01,740 --> 00:08:04,080
But not everyone was reassured.

94
00:08:04,081 --> 00:08:09,940
After all, there was something menacing about the stature of big buildings.

95
00:08:10,240 --> 00:08:14,920
They seemed to overpower people like some science fiction prophecy come true.

96
00:08:15,740 --> 00:08:18,200
Were we really entering the ominous future

97
00:08:18,201 --> 00:08:21,500
proclaimed by Fritz Lang's terrifying metropolis?

98
00:08:22,380 --> 00:08:25,940
What will the world be like when buildings grew so large

99
00:08:25,941 --> 00:08:27,640
they blocked out the sun?

100
00:08:28,640 --> 00:08:31,180
This fear led to more zoning regulations

101
00:08:31,181 --> 00:08:34,460
and to architectural setbacks in a building's design,

102
00:08:34,960 --> 00:08:38,880
ensuring a progressively narrower structure as more height was achieved

103
00:08:38,881 --> 00:08:42,160
and guaranteeing pedestrians a view of the sky.

104
00:08:42,940 --> 00:08:45,840
After the technical issues of construction had been addressed,

105
00:08:46,200 --> 00:08:50,340
the main reason for New York finally adopting the concept of tall buildings

106
00:08:50,341 --> 00:08:53,120
was the egos of the men who wanted them built.

107
00:08:54,040 --> 00:08:59,420
After all, the largest buildings in a city are always the ones that hold the greatest power.

108
00:08:59,421 --> 00:09:08,380
In the past, houses of worship owned the skyline, but in 1913, New York developed a broader interpretation of the theme.

109
00:09:09,500 --> 00:09:18,540
Frank Woolworth spent his own nickels dimes to create an ornate 60-story Gothic structure that lay claim to the mantle of the world's tallest building.

110
00:09:19,680 --> 00:09:23,380
The Woolworth building remains the most romantic of all the buildings.

111
00:09:23,381 --> 00:09:31,140
It does what a skyscraper ought to do, which was to aspire to heaven, to go up and up and up, and to be like a cathedral.

112
00:09:31,640 --> 00:09:43,745
And therefore it was chosen to have bestowed on it a kind of gothic dress, and people thought of it as a cathedral, but a cathedral of commerce instead of a cathedral

113
00:09:43,746 --> 00:09:44,620
of religion.

114
00:09:45,980 --> 00:09:53,120
Suddenly the race was on among the rich and powerful to build the ultimate edifice in the modern age of business titans.

115
00:09:53,380 --> 00:10:01,920
A new word was coined for these ambitious spires to the clouds, skyscraper, a term previously reserved for tall ships.

116
00:10:03,820 --> 00:10:12,160
Architects reveled in their new notoriety, throwing wild costume parties where they would lay claim to their audacious design achievements.

117
00:10:12,940 --> 00:10:18,280
In 1929, there were over 700 buildings that were at least 20 stories tall.

118
00:10:19,520 --> 00:10:22,760
New York was fast becoming an armada of skyscrapers.

119
00:10:23,380 --> 00:10:26,220
but storm clouds were rising on Wall Street.

120
00:10:26,980 --> 00:10:28,440
There were rough seas ahead.

121
00:10:30,260 --> 00:10:35,000
The architect of the Empire State Building could not attend the dedication of the building.

122
00:10:35,340 --> 00:10:38,420
Instead, he sent a telegram from his ship en route to England.

123
00:10:38,880 --> 00:10:41,300
One day out, and I can still see the building.

124
00:10:42,140 --> 00:10:44,320
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

125
00:10:46,160 --> 00:10:50,320
The 1920s were a time to throw caution to the wind,

126
00:10:50,321 --> 00:10:52,600
with wild exuberant abandon.

127
00:10:53,380 --> 00:11:02,380
The greatest irony of the Roaring Twenties was prohibition, a nationally sanctioned moral hypocrisy.

128
00:11:03,940 --> 00:11:10,940
Alcohol is hindering the coming of world peace because it is befuddling the thinking of humanity.

129
00:11:11,880 --> 00:11:15,380
People were labeled by their position on the issue of alcohol.

130
00:11:15,820 --> 00:11:20,420
Some were considered dry, others who openly endorsed consumption were called wet.

131
00:11:21,100 --> 00:11:22,820
Al Smith was wet.

132
00:11:23,380 --> 00:11:30,040
I will not be influenced in appointments by the question of a person's wet or dry attitude.

133
00:11:31,320 --> 00:11:36,500
And that was a factor in his losing the 1928 presidential election to Herbert Hoover.

134
00:11:37,720 --> 00:11:41,380
Smith had linked all his political ambitions to the race for the White House,

135
00:11:41,600 --> 00:11:45,880
so that for the first time in 25 years he was out of public office.

136
00:11:46,440 --> 00:11:50,220
But these were the roaring 20s, and the music played on.

137
00:11:50,221 --> 00:11:54,060
mass production was in full swing

138
00:11:54,061 --> 00:11:55,180
people danced

139
00:11:55,181 --> 00:11:58,380
bootleg booze was flowing like tap water

140
00:11:58,381 --> 00:12:00,040
people danced

141
00:12:00,041 --> 00:12:03,720
the jazz sound drew a sharp cultural line

142
00:12:03,721 --> 00:12:04,960
between generations

143
00:12:04,961 --> 00:12:06,860
people danced

144
00:12:06,861 --> 00:12:14,060
but when wall street collapsed

145
00:12:14,061 --> 00:12:17,240
the dancing stopped

146
00:12:17,920 --> 00:12:22,100
The Depression brought a grinding halt to any sort of festivities

147
00:12:22,101 --> 00:12:25,900
and an abrupt end to New York's high-rise building boom.

148
00:12:26,620 --> 00:12:30,240
The situation in New York was particularly bleak.

149
00:12:30,820 --> 00:12:33,440
You can't grow food on a concrete landscape.

150
00:12:34,220 --> 00:12:37,080
You can't find work when business has collapsed.

151
00:12:37,840 --> 00:12:41,060
People hoped for signs that this was only a temporary setback,

152
00:12:41,061 --> 00:12:46,800
that the grand proposition called the American Dream wasn't a paper moon over a cardboard sea.

153
00:12:47,240 --> 00:12:52,680
As both a commercial venture and an investment in the resilient spirit of New York,

154
00:12:52,800 --> 00:12:55,380
the few men who had money defied the Depression

155
00:12:55,381 --> 00:13:01,260
and hired the scrappy Al Smith to spearhead the greatest urban construction project ever.

156
00:13:02,160 --> 00:13:07,420
Their plan was to erect the world's largest building as a practical monument

157
00:13:07,421 --> 00:13:10,780
to a culture they hoped would weather the hard times.

158
00:13:11,260 --> 00:13:14,580
The design came from New York architect William Lamb,

159
00:13:14,581 --> 00:13:16,080
who was asked a simple question,

160
00:13:16,081 --> 00:13:19,180
How big can you build it so it won't fall down?

161
00:13:19,960 --> 00:13:24,020
John Raskin, founder of General Motors and primary backer of the project,

162
00:13:24,160 --> 00:13:28,980
suggested a design based on the shape of the thick pencils favored by their young children.

163
00:13:29,760 --> 00:13:32,660
Lamb's final design incorporated this idea.

164
00:13:33,040 --> 00:13:37,480
They would call it the Empire State, New York's traditional nickname.

165
00:13:37,780 --> 00:13:39,700
It would become a legend.

166
00:13:46,080 --> 00:13:53,240
The site of the Empire State Building is, or was, the site at the corner of 5th Avenue

167
00:13:53,241 --> 00:13:59,300
and 34th Street of the Waldorf Astoria Hotel, once the most popular and fashionable hotel

168
00:13:59,301 --> 00:14:00,420
in New York City.

169
00:14:00,421 --> 00:14:03,180
It was no longer a fashionable area by 1931.

170
00:14:03,660 --> 00:14:05,200
The hotel moved uptown.

171
00:14:05,400 --> 00:14:09,820
The old building was torn down, and the Empire State Building went up in its place.

172
00:14:09,821 --> 00:14:14,320
The officers and directors of Empire State Incorporated

173
00:14:14,321 --> 00:14:18,320
are on the roof of the old Waldorf Astoria Hotel

174
00:14:18,321 --> 00:14:22,500
about to begin demolition of that ancient and historic structure.

175
00:14:23,440 --> 00:14:27,960
Now gentlemen, stand back while I start the real work of demolition.

176
00:14:28,240 --> 00:14:32,040
With that, Al Smith ceremoniously pulled down the trappings

177
00:14:32,041 --> 00:14:34,360
of the exclusive Waldorf Astoria Hotel

178
00:14:34,361 --> 00:14:37,600
to clear the way for the Empire State Building.

179
00:14:37,601 --> 00:14:38,820
It was quite a moment.

180
00:14:38,821 --> 00:14:44,500
the favorite son from the Lower East Side knocking away a symbol of economic segregation

181
00:14:44,501 --> 00:14:49,980
erected by the wealthy to replace it with a towering vision of a new America.

182
00:14:50,740 --> 00:14:56,160
I went to work during the demolition of the old Waldorf Astoria Hotel.

183
00:14:56,440 --> 00:14:57,460
I was 16 then.

184
00:14:58,040 --> 00:15:01,240
The workers were going through this old fancy hotel, you know,

185
00:15:01,840 --> 00:15:03,260
and a couple of them came down and said,

186
00:15:03,680 --> 00:15:06,560
Look, they got urinals for everybody.

187
00:15:07,500 --> 00:15:11,220
They were talking about the bidettes that were in those fancy hotel rooms.

188
00:15:13,160 --> 00:15:16,900
The future must use the past as its foundation,

189
00:15:17,160 --> 00:15:21,220
and the foundation for the Empire State is a history measured in contrasts.

190
00:15:22,540 --> 00:15:26,440
A century prior to its days as high society's Waldorf Astoria,

191
00:15:26,720 --> 00:15:28,280
playground for the rich and famous,

192
00:15:28,920 --> 00:15:32,780
34th Street and 5th Avenue had been the site of a family farm.

193
00:15:33,220 --> 00:15:35,880
Before Manhattan looked anything like it does now,

194
00:15:35,881 --> 00:15:36,726
it was farmland.

195
00:15:36,727 --> 00:15:41,060
And there used to be a farm on this site owned by a gentleman named John Thompson.

196
00:15:41,460 --> 00:15:46,020
And there was a stream and he had cows and, you know, all the normal farm things.

197
00:15:46,840 --> 00:15:54,560
As America claimed its independence in 1776, this was the site for a spectacularly unsuccessful

198
00:15:54,561 --> 00:15:56,246
revolutionary war battle.

199
00:15:56,247 --> 00:16:00,900
There was a great deal of battling going on all around New York City.

200
00:16:00,901 --> 00:16:05,940
the British landed here with 45,000 troops, which is an enormous number.

201
00:16:06,060 --> 00:16:09,060
We only had, we Americans, had 8,000 troops.

202
00:16:09,380 --> 00:16:13,560
And Washington was having to fight a delaying action all the time,

203
00:16:13,640 --> 00:16:17,740
retreating all the way across what was the rocky little island of Manhattan.

204
00:16:18,300 --> 00:16:24,360
Stories persist that the spot where George Washington tried to physically quit his men from their hasty retreat

205
00:16:24,361 --> 00:16:28,180
is exactly where the Empire State Building stands today.

206
00:16:28,920 --> 00:16:32,300
New York City remained in possession of the British all during the Revolution,

207
00:16:32,940 --> 00:16:36,720
and I'm sorry to say that the natives of New York were very glad to have the British there.

208
00:16:36,800 --> 00:16:39,980
They had lots of parties, they made lots of money off the visitors,

209
00:16:39,981 --> 00:16:45,040
and they were somewhat chagrined when it turned out that we, after all, had won the war instead of lost it.

210
00:16:45,960 --> 00:16:50,860
The geological foundation of the Empire State Building is millions of years old,

211
00:16:51,160 --> 00:16:55,480
a broad expanse of pure granite bedrock stretching beneath Manhattan.

212
00:16:55,481 --> 00:17:00,480
This section of North America's continental plate is nature's sturdiest structural support

213
00:17:01,020 --> 00:17:03,300
and would make skyscrapers possible.

214
00:17:04,500 --> 00:17:07,440
Bedrock has largely defined the New York skyline

215
00:17:07,441 --> 00:17:10,880
and ensures that Manhattan will never sink under the weight of its buildings.

216
00:17:11,320 --> 00:17:16,220
But some say the Empire State Building is also protected by the luck of the Irish,

217
00:17:16,680 --> 00:17:20,538
since Al Smith arranged for construction to commence on St.

218
00:17:20,539 --> 00:17:22,500
Patrick's Day, March 17, 1930,

219
00:17:22,501 --> 00:17:25,320
less than two months after excavation.

220
00:17:25,480 --> 00:17:32,480
The base of the building is two square acres of solid concrete laid 55 feet below sea level.

221
00:17:32,481 --> 00:17:39,480
From this foundation 60,000 tons of steel frame would extend the building to a height of 1,454 feet,

222
00:17:39,481 --> 00:17:45,480
over 102 stories high, more than a quarter mile into the clouds.

223
00:17:45,481 --> 00:17:51,480
By the time Al Smith laid the cornerstone on September 17th, only eight months after the project began,

224
00:17:51,481 --> 00:17:55,480
The estimated construction cost was 25 million dollars,

225
00:17:55,481 --> 00:18:00,480
half the projected figure due to the effect of the depression on the value of the dollar.

226
00:18:03,480 --> 00:18:08,480
Well, I think everybody was grateful for the fact that any amount of building was going on in the depression in New York City,

227
00:18:08,481 --> 00:18:14,480
which is why the Empire State Building was continuously looked up to and reported on in the course of its construction.

228
00:18:14,481 --> 00:18:19,480
It also was very rapidly built because everybody was eager to be doing work.

229
00:18:21,480 --> 00:18:22,866
The pace was frantic.

230
00:18:22,867 --> 00:18:27,880
New innovations and prefabricated materials efficiently assembled by workers

231
00:18:27,881 --> 00:18:31,700
allowed the structure to grow an average of four and a half stories a week.

232
00:18:31,920 --> 00:18:38,060
In one astonishing 10-day period, the building grows a record 14 floors.

233
00:18:39,180 --> 00:18:43,020
But the real challenge wasn't speed, it was logistics.

234
00:18:43,860 --> 00:18:50,660
The steel for the structure, more than a pound for every man, woman and child then living in the United States, was made in Pittsburgh.

235
00:18:51,480 --> 00:18:57,960
enormous beams fashion to rigid specifications the 44-ton columns then

236
00:18:57,961 --> 00:19:03,180
loaded onto railroad cars to be shipped nearly 400 miles to the center of

237
00:19:03,181 --> 00:19:08,360
Manhattan reaching there about three days later

238
00:19:09,560 --> 00:19:14,360
the delivery timetable was exacting because when you're building in the

239
00:19:14,361 --> 00:19:19,100
middle of a bustling city there's no place to store construction materials

240
00:19:19,101 --> 00:19:23,460
So when a steel beam arrived, it had to be erected immediately.

241
00:19:24,320 --> 00:19:26,580
There was simply no place to set it aside.

242
00:19:29,480 --> 00:19:35,660
The construction team devised an elaborate system of hoists and internal transport systems

243
00:19:35,661 --> 00:19:38,580
to distribute materials throughout the building site.

244
00:19:39,420 --> 00:19:42,860
Each piece of steel was carefully marked to show its destination.

245
00:19:42,861 --> 00:19:49,860
Then, using cables and pulleys, it was hoisted into position through a series of cranes or derricks.

246
00:19:49,861 --> 00:19:55,860
These cranes were then lifted to higher levels as the steel work rose around them.

247
00:19:55,861 --> 00:20:03,860
For an extra measure of speed, temporary rail tracks were installed on each floor to transport smaller materials.

248
00:20:03,861 --> 00:20:11,860
Meanwhile, up on the high iron, something you don't see anymore, riveters tossing white-hot steel pellets through the air.

249
00:20:11,861 --> 00:20:12,561
the air.

250
00:20:12,562 --> 00:20:19,060
I'm old enough to remember watching guys take the rivets out of the hot bucket and he'll

251
00:20:19,061 --> 00:20:23,320
throw it up to the other guy while it's white hot and he'll stick it in that open hole and he'll

252
00:20:23,321 --> 00:20:27,341
pound it home and that's the way they built the Empire State Building.

253
00:20:27,342 --> 00:20:28,720
Hot rivets can be molded

254
00:20:28,721 --> 00:20:33,340
into the shape of the connecting holes and when they cool and contract they tighten the bond

255
00:20:33,341 --> 00:20:38,914
between the girders. Today bolts and screws are the more economic alternative.

256
00:20:38,915 --> 00:20:40,200
Being part of this

257
00:20:40,201 --> 00:20:44,024
process infused the men with a sense of ownership in the building.

258
00:20:44,025 --> 00:20:45,820
They had a special camaraderie,

259
00:20:45,821 --> 00:20:53,380
and their pride resonates today, whether they laid plaster, bumped cement, installed radiators,

260
00:20:53,940 --> 00:20:55,238
or handled the steel.

261
00:20:55,239 --> 00:21:00,060
I've ran into some people that claim they worked on the Empire State Building.

262
00:21:00,480 --> 00:21:05,817
I said, what were you? Oh, I was a plumber. I said, when? He says, 1931.

263
00:21:05,818 --> 00:21:07,300
I said, oh, come on,

264
00:21:07,301 --> 00:21:11,000
forget you didn't work on the Empire State Building during construction.

265
00:21:11,001 --> 00:21:11,720
You were after

266
00:21:11,721 --> 00:21:14,520
construction. And please, will you mention that in the future?

267
00:21:16,260 --> 00:21:22,140
Fourteen workers fell to their deaths over the construction period as the web of steel

268
00:21:22,141 --> 00:21:27,992
frame climbed higher and higher. Meanwhile, interior work raced to keep pace.

269
00:21:27,993 --> 00:21:28,693
Eighteen

270
00:21:28,694 --> 00:21:33,400
truckloads of elevator equipment arrived daily for a separate crew of three hundred and forty

271
00:21:33,401 --> 00:21:42,400
men to install 73 elevators operating in seven miles of shafts, 10 million bricks, 730 tons

272
00:21:42,960 --> 00:21:47,844
of aluminum and stainless steel molding were attached to the exterior.

273
00:21:47,845 --> 00:21:49,240
50 miles of radiator

274
00:21:49,241 --> 00:21:56,800
pipe, 70 miles of water pipe, 2.5 million feet of electrical wire, 1,060 miles of telephone

275
00:21:56,801 --> 00:21:59,310
cable weave through the building.

276
00:21:59,311 --> 00:22:03,340
6,500 windows form the glass mosaic on the building's

277
00:22:03,341 --> 00:22:10,340
7 million man hours of work were performed by crews which swelled to nearly 4,000 men.

278
00:22:10,341 --> 00:22:18,340
In record time, the Empire State Building was finally completed in just one year and 45 days.

279
00:22:18,341 --> 00:22:25,340
They dug the foundations on St. Patrick's Day, 1930.

280
00:22:25,341 --> 00:22:30,340
And they kept the building 1931.

281
00:22:30,341 --> 00:22:32,880
today it takes that long to change a light bulb.

282
00:22:34,320 --> 00:22:38,640
There was a price to pay for rushing construction on the Empire State Building.

283
00:22:39,080 --> 00:22:40,500
Fourteen men died.

284
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:44,380
The Chrysler Building, built more slowly, lost only one worker.

285
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:46,580
Modern Marvels will return.

286
00:22:49,560 --> 00:22:55,940
1929, yes, the times were hard, but I had worked as a water boy.

287
00:22:55,941 --> 00:23:00,620
I had my mother and three brothers and a sister to take care of.

288
00:23:01,120 --> 00:23:05,740
We lived in Brooklyn, so I had to get up at 6 o'clock in the morning,

289
00:23:06,640 --> 00:23:09,240
have a quick breakfast with a roll, a cup of coffee.

290
00:23:09,320 --> 00:23:10,840
That was all we could afford at that time.

291
00:23:11,360 --> 00:23:15,640
And then take the Nickel Subway to 34th Street.

292
00:23:16,880 --> 00:23:22,300
To me, it was a big adventure at 16, 17 years old.

293
00:23:22,360 --> 00:23:23,060
Come on.

294
00:23:23,480 --> 00:23:25,340
I used to make a lot of money in that time.

295
00:23:25,940 --> 00:23:31,620
five dollars a week plus maybe five six seven eight nine dollars a week in tips from the steel workers

296
00:23:31,621 --> 00:23:33,240
because they used to throw a little

297
00:23:33,241 --> 00:23:35,140
lemonade into their water

298
00:23:35,141 --> 00:23:36,340
at the end of the week

299
00:23:36,341 --> 00:23:38,920
you put your water boy put in a bucket

300
00:23:38,921 --> 00:23:40,140
by the paymaster

301
00:23:40,141 --> 00:23:42,520
whatever the change they had in their pockets

302
00:23:42,521 --> 00:23:43,720
they would throw

303
00:23:43,721 --> 00:23:45,000
in the bucket

304
00:23:45,001 --> 00:23:48,560
every man on the job was grateful for a paycheck

305
00:23:48,561 --> 00:23:52,160
some steel workers made as much as two hundred dollars a month

306
00:23:52,161 --> 00:23:59,600
But an equally valuable experience for many of the workers was the chance to learn from their co-workers, like the Mohawk Indians.

307
00:24:00,960 --> 00:24:04,760
The Mohawks, they were the skywalkers, yes.

308
00:24:05,700 --> 00:24:09,180
Because whenever there was something big going up, they were there.

309
00:24:09,820 --> 00:24:18,000
Since the mid-1800s, these Native Americans have made high-altitude construction work a part of their tribal rite of passage.

310
00:24:18,001 --> 00:24:26,880
They see it as a journey into self and a denial of death linked to the tradition of courage and grace established by their ancestors.

311
00:24:27,740 --> 00:24:32,040
The Mohawks who worked on the Empire State Building live in what is now Quebec.

312
00:24:32,920 --> 00:24:42,720
But many spent their lives on a sacred pilgrimage from construction site to construction site, practicing the aerial ritual of connecting girder to girder.

313
00:24:44,220 --> 00:24:45,680
They were fantastic.

314
00:24:45,681 --> 00:24:49,340
They walked across those steel beams as if they were nothing.

315
00:24:50,160 --> 00:24:53,760
And as I was a young fella, they took me under their wing.

316
00:24:54,680 --> 00:24:57,080
And they told me how to do it, how to do it, how to do it.

317
00:24:57,460 --> 00:25:00,460
Look, Joe, look across there.

318
00:25:00,940 --> 00:25:01,840
Don't look down.

319
00:25:02,140 --> 00:25:03,700
Look straight across where you're walking.

320
00:25:03,860 --> 00:25:05,260
Put one foot behind the other.

321
00:25:05,760 --> 00:25:08,000
And we will be behind you if you make a mistake.

322
00:25:08,880 --> 00:25:10,640
And I'll tell you, I learned to walk those beams.

323
00:25:11,340 --> 00:25:15,180
And I never made a mistake until one time I stepped on a greasy girder.

324
00:25:15,680 --> 00:25:17,420
And it slipped on me.

325
00:25:18,080 --> 00:25:19,700
That was not my fault or their fault.

326
00:25:20,060 --> 00:25:21,540
There was a bit of grease on it.

327
00:25:21,900 --> 00:25:24,420
But I'll tell you, they were there so fast,

328
00:25:24,700 --> 00:25:28,680
I don't think I was on that grid of more than 15 seconds before they had me off.

329
00:25:29,540 --> 00:25:32,140
But they said they had to pry me off that beam.

330
00:25:32,960 --> 00:25:33,880
But I wasn't hurt.

331
00:25:33,960 --> 00:25:35,580
I just scraped my legs a little bit.

332
00:25:36,240 --> 00:25:39,320
And then I went downstairs and I told the woman,

333
00:25:39,520 --> 00:25:40,620
I'm not going back up there.

334
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:42,000
He says, the hell you're not.

335
00:25:42,860 --> 00:25:44,780
He says, you're going back up there or you're fired.

336
00:25:45,680 --> 00:25:46,600
I said, why?

337
00:25:47,600 --> 00:25:49,140
He said, if you don't go back up there tomorrow,

338
00:25:49,460 --> 00:25:50,680
you're never going back there today,

339
00:25:50,860 --> 00:25:52,080
you're never going to go back there tomorrow.

340
00:25:54,460 --> 00:25:55,620
I'll tell you, he was good.

341
00:25:56,320 --> 00:25:58,280
He really proved something to me.

342
00:25:58,540 --> 00:26:01,740
Look, you don't back away from something.

343
00:26:02,980 --> 00:26:05,040
And I've never backed away from anything since.

344
00:26:06,240 --> 00:26:08,820
While the Mohawks had their eyes on the heavens,

345
00:26:08,821 --> 00:26:13,340
Al Smith had his eyes focused on a competing construction project

346
00:26:13,341 --> 00:26:15,040
a few short blocks away.

347
00:26:15,041 --> 00:26:17,100
the brand new Chrysler building.

348
00:26:17,760 --> 00:26:20,700
To some, the Chrysler building had a more contemporary look

349
00:26:20,701 --> 00:26:23,720
that played off the image of its automotive backer.

350
00:26:24,320 --> 00:26:28,880
Traditional Gothic gargoyles were replaced with stylized hood ornaments.

351
00:26:29,540 --> 00:26:32,740
The surface was festooned with hubcap medallions.

352
00:26:33,580 --> 00:26:35,440
But far more disturbing for Al Smith

353
00:26:35,441 --> 00:26:39,740
was that the building proved to be an architectural Trojan horse.

354
00:26:40,320 --> 00:26:44,300
The Art Deco spire was secretly constructed inside the building

355
00:26:44,301 --> 00:26:49,820
and erected as a surprise addition, bringing its height to 1,046 feet,

356
00:26:50,240 --> 00:26:54,080
only four feet short of the Empire State's proposed height.

357
00:26:54,540 --> 00:27:00,140
So Al Smith and his board of directors insisted their building also wear a hat.

358
00:27:00,141 --> 00:27:05,400
They would build an observatory and dirigible mooring mass 200 more feet.

359
00:27:06,020 --> 00:27:09,140
That, the Chrysler Building, could never surpass.

360
00:27:09,680 --> 00:27:11,340
These are celebratory tops.

361
00:27:11,341 --> 00:27:14,600
They express a willful human gesture.

362
00:27:14,840 --> 00:27:18,540
They remind you that, okay, maybe a machine made it,

363
00:27:18,600 --> 00:27:20,880
but a man designed it, and a man had the last word,

364
00:27:20,960 --> 00:27:24,300
and he put this wonderful little signature on the top of it.

365
00:27:24,620 --> 00:27:29,220
It sort of reasserts the importance of the human touch.

366
00:27:29,820 --> 00:27:34,560
There are stories that Al Smith actually climbed to the top of the Empire State Building

367
00:27:34,561 --> 00:27:36,460
the night before its grand opening

368
00:27:36,461 --> 00:27:40,560
and left his signature somewhere inside the hi-hat he inspired.

369
00:27:40,561 --> 00:27:45,220
At that point, he was not a politician. He was not a businessman.

370
00:27:45,660 --> 00:27:50,040
He was just a kid from the Lower East Side who had reached the top.

371
00:27:50,420 --> 00:27:56,120
And as he left his humble mark of graffiti, he forever linked himself to the American dream.

372
00:27:58,220 --> 00:28:01,900
There are 6,500 windows in the Empire State Building.

373
00:28:02,200 --> 00:28:04,120
All get cleaned twice a month.

374
00:28:04,700 --> 00:28:07,560
Window washers qualify for combat pay.

375
00:28:07,561 --> 00:28:11,040
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

376
00:28:12,540 --> 00:28:17,340
Governor, we are on the top of the 86th floor of the Empire State Building.

377
00:28:18,200 --> 00:28:25,240
The highest point in the world today that can be reached from a building designed,

378
00:28:26,140 --> 00:28:31,540
fashioned, built by the brain, the bronze, the ingenuity, and the muscle of mankind.

379
00:28:31,541 --> 00:28:38,540
I am very happy to take part in this very auspicious occasion with my old friend, Governor Smith.

380
00:28:39,040 --> 00:28:44,180
On May 1st, 1931, after a VIP ceremony atop the building,

381
00:28:44,800 --> 00:28:50,140
Al Smith gave his grandchildren the honor of cutting the ceremonial ribbon across the front doors.

382
00:28:50,800 --> 00:28:57,700
Hundreds of miles away in Washington, D.C., President Hoover pressed a button that lit the power of the world's largest building,

383
00:28:57,701 --> 00:29:00,920
and the Empire State Building was open for business.

384
00:29:01,540 --> 00:29:05,020
When it was finished, it was almost totally unoccupied.

385
00:29:05,560 --> 00:29:06,380
There were no tenants.

386
00:29:07,240 --> 00:29:11,280
I can remember the governor, Al Smith.

387
00:29:11,520 --> 00:29:16,620
I can remember him very well, jumping on his cigar and wearing his little brown derby.

388
00:29:17,160 --> 00:29:19,380
We won first name calling.

389
00:29:19,480 --> 00:29:21,280
He called me Jack, and I called him governor,

390
00:29:21,640 --> 00:29:24,520
because we were about the only two people in the building.

391
00:29:25,900 --> 00:29:28,360
There were very few other tenants.

392
00:29:28,361 --> 00:29:30,220
They had boards in the lobby.

393
00:29:30,221 --> 00:29:32,220
The terrazzo hadn't even been put in.

394
00:29:32,960 --> 00:29:36,440
The building was only opened up to the 20th floor on the inside.

395
00:29:36,700 --> 00:29:38,680
Of course, the outside was completed.

396
00:29:39,420 --> 00:29:42,680
At first, the building's high-tech, dirigible mooring station

397
00:29:42,681 --> 00:29:45,620
was seen as an attraction for international merchants

398
00:29:45,621 --> 00:29:48,940
who traveled on those giant European airships.

399
00:29:49,280 --> 00:29:55,000
Unfortunately, people had overlooked the fact that New York has canyons,

400
00:29:55,260 --> 00:29:56,880
man-made canyons, but canyons.

401
00:29:57,080 --> 00:29:59,180
And in canyons, you have wind.

402
00:29:59,181 --> 00:30:02,800
so that the two times the dirigibles were moored here,

403
00:30:03,260 --> 00:30:05,940
or there was an attempt made to moor the dirigibles,

404
00:30:06,380 --> 00:30:07,080
they were upended.

405
00:30:08,360 --> 00:30:12,880
Al Smith called reporters to cover the first airship delivery of the Daily News.

406
00:30:12,881 --> 00:30:14,440
It was dropped by rope,

407
00:30:14,940 --> 00:30:17,440
but celebrities attending the event had to scramble

408
00:30:17,441 --> 00:30:19,560
to avoid getting swept off the building.

409
00:30:20,160 --> 00:30:23,240
The darn thing went up and stood almost vertically on its nose

410
00:30:23,241 --> 00:30:25,640
before they were able to get it back down.

411
00:30:25,641 --> 00:30:30,300
The 1937 explosion of the Hindenburg at Lakehurst, New Jersey,

412
00:30:30,301 --> 00:30:35,740
finally convinced management that dirigibles had no place over the Empire State Building.

413
00:30:40,960 --> 00:30:46,900
Smith, meanwhile, was trying to attract tenants with any kind of publicity stunt he could come up with.

414
00:30:50,120 --> 00:30:55,200
Well, I don't know about the 10-gallon hat, but the accompaniment is okay.

415
00:30:55,640 --> 00:31:01,220
But rooftop press conferences and stunts weren't enough to lure people to the building.

416
00:31:01,940 --> 00:31:07,380
Ironically, it was not beauty, but a beast who would capture the world's imagination.

417
00:31:07,381 --> 00:31:21,381
When it became the scene of the great climactic part of the movie King Kong,

418
00:31:25,720 --> 00:31:28,380
it became immortal all over the world.

419
00:31:28,560 --> 00:31:30,640
So the people all over the world came to New York,

420
00:31:31,040 --> 00:31:34,540
bent upon seeing the Empire State Building if they saw nothing else.

421
00:31:35,100 --> 00:31:39,120
At last, the Empire State Building was becoming fashionable.

422
00:31:40,240 --> 00:31:43,820
Particularly this neighborhood became the center of the fashion and garment industry.

423
00:31:44,540 --> 00:31:48,660
That was really the bulk of our tenants, were fashion and garment related industries.

424
00:31:48,661 --> 00:31:53,220
And the building filled rapidly and it became an address that people sought.

425
00:31:53,960 --> 00:31:58,860
We used to have an ad that said, you don't need the address, just say it's the Empire State Building.

426
00:31:58,861 --> 00:32:04,680
With the Empire State as its emblem, New York once again assumed its big city crown

427
00:32:04,681 --> 00:32:10,780
and committed itself to the future, offering to lead the nation into the world of tomorrow.

428
00:32:11,340 --> 00:32:16,620
But America would soon realize that the world of tomorrow was a world at war,

429
00:32:17,520 --> 00:32:25,140
a nightmare of sneak attacks, death camps, tyrants, and terror.

430
00:32:25,940 --> 00:32:27,920
Yet through these tumultuous times,

431
00:32:28,060 --> 00:32:30,620
the Empire State Building seemed to project stability

432
00:32:30,621 --> 00:32:33,500
and reassurance of the nation's strength.

433
00:32:34,360 --> 00:32:39,420
I think many people saw this as a symbol of hope,

434
00:32:40,100 --> 00:32:44,000
as a symbol of what America could do

435
00:32:44,001 --> 00:32:46,620
and what America would come back to.

436
00:32:47,500 --> 00:32:50,520
That stability was literally put to the test

437
00:32:50,521 --> 00:32:52,540
on a foggy morning in 45.

438
00:32:52,541 --> 00:32:57,140
The Empire State Building and all New York City were wrapped in fog

439
00:32:57,141 --> 00:33:00,940
as a B-25 Mitchell bomber trying to reach a nearby airport

440
00:33:00,941 --> 00:33:03,500
crashed into the tallest structure in the world.

441
00:33:04,160 --> 00:33:07,160
Thirteen were killed, including the three in the plane.

442
00:33:09,760 --> 00:33:14,240
The Empire State Building's 79th floor was hardest hit by the crash

443
00:33:14,241 --> 00:33:16,700
after the bomber's pilot lost his bearings

444
00:33:16,701 --> 00:33:20,100
and tried unsuccessfully to bank away from the skyscraper.

445
00:33:20,101 --> 00:33:28,100
With elevators out of order, firemen had to drag their hoses many stories high to fight the flames spread by gasoline and oil.

446
00:33:28,101 --> 00:33:31,100
Disaster units respond to the emergency call.

447
00:33:35,100 --> 00:33:40,100
When the plane hit the building's north side, parts of it were jammed into the walls.

448
00:33:40,101 --> 00:33:42,100
Others plummeted down elevator shafts.

449
00:33:42,101 --> 00:33:49,100
Still others crashed through seven consecutive walls and out the south side, causing damage to nearby buildings.

450
00:33:49,101 --> 00:33:54,400
One elevator fell 80 floors, but its girl operator miraculously escaped death.

451
00:33:56,980 --> 00:34:02,940
950 feet above the street, repairs are begun on New York's 102-story skyscraper.

452
00:34:07,040 --> 00:34:11,480
I called my office and the girl that answered the phone was hysterical.

453
00:34:11,620 --> 00:34:13,240
They said, they're bombing the building.

454
00:34:14,100 --> 00:34:15,180
So I said, who's bombing?

455
00:34:15,340 --> 00:34:16,360
She said, the Japs.

456
00:34:16,361 --> 00:34:19,960
So I said, where are they bombing it from?

457
00:34:20,040 --> 00:34:22,960
She says, I don't know, but she says the building's burning down.

458
00:34:23,220 --> 00:34:25,460
And I said, put somebody else on them.

459
00:34:25,520 --> 00:34:27,460
I thought she would have gone berserk.

460
00:34:27,800 --> 00:34:32,760
You see there's a map below the socks which shows you where the building is,

461
00:34:32,840 --> 00:34:35,480
which is on the corner of 34th and 5th Avenue.

462
00:34:36,000 --> 00:34:41,200
Our office is approximately halfway up on the 66th floor,

463
00:34:41,201 --> 00:34:46,420
and the plane hit the building on the 34th Street side

464
00:34:46,421 --> 00:34:51,140
right up here at the 79th floor.

465
00:34:52,340 --> 00:34:58,460
The office it hit was the Catholic Charities office on the 79th floor

466
00:34:58,461 --> 00:35:02,800
and I don't believe anybody was working there on Saturday.

467
00:35:03,260 --> 00:35:05,020
Fortunately, it happened on a Saturday.

468
00:35:05,900 --> 00:35:10,500
A few decades earlier, there was a fear that wind could topple tall structures.

469
00:35:10,501 --> 00:35:16,040
The Empire State Building proved its solid construction could withstand far more than that.

470
00:35:16,500 --> 00:35:22,140
With the tragedy of the airplane collision, the public's perception of the building finally found a consensus.

471
00:35:22,840 --> 00:35:27,080
The Empire State was no longer a novelty, a new kid on the block.

472
00:35:27,280 --> 00:35:34,920
It was now a full-fledged New Yorker, big, bold, confident, with uptown style and downtown attitude.

473
00:35:35,580 --> 00:35:37,620
I always look at the building like a ship.

474
00:35:37,621 --> 00:35:42,420
It's big, two and a quarter million square feet.

475
00:35:42,421 --> 00:35:46,780
It certainly has two and a quarter million people coming to the observatory every year

476
00:35:46,781 --> 00:35:49,220
and 20,000 people a day to come here to do business.

477
00:35:49,221 --> 00:35:57,620
This modern marvel is such a part of New York that today it feels as though the Empire State Building has always been there.

478
00:35:57,621 --> 00:36:03,120
The Empire State Building is one of the greatest lightning rods ever built,

479
00:36:03,121 --> 00:36:08,740
allegedly when conditions are right and the 86th floor is surrounded by static electricity

480
00:36:08,741 --> 00:36:14,200
if you kiss someone fire will fly from your lips modern marvels will be right back

481
00:36:14,201 --> 00:36:21,380
there's a wonderful story about the three men that are laying bricks they're asked well what

482
00:36:21,381 --> 00:36:27,120
are you what are you doing here and the first man says uh make a living they asked the second man

483
00:36:27,121 --> 00:36:30,020
and he says, building a wall.

484
00:36:31,300 --> 00:36:33,280
The third man, when asked the question,

485
00:36:33,380 --> 00:36:35,660
what are you doing, answers, I'm building a cathedral.

486
00:36:36,280 --> 00:36:38,380
For the men who built the Empire State Building,

487
00:36:38,500 --> 00:36:40,820
making a living was foremost in their minds.

488
00:36:40,821 --> 00:36:43,540
But building a monument with a vision of tomorrow

489
00:36:43,541 --> 00:36:44,620
was in their hearts.

490
00:36:45,120 --> 00:36:47,460
Their commitment to America and its future

491
00:36:47,461 --> 00:36:49,780
is the very soul of the Empire State,

492
00:36:50,000 --> 00:36:51,780
a modern city within a city,

493
00:36:51,940 --> 00:36:54,580
and their dreams and dedication are celebrated

494
00:36:54,581 --> 00:36:57,360
in the art deco design of the five-story lobby.

495
00:36:58,620 --> 00:37:03,340
Giant bronze medallions proclaim the contributions of various craftspeople,

496
00:37:03,341 --> 00:37:08,820
and a towering metal mosaic positions the building as the center of the universe.

497
00:37:09,440 --> 00:37:12,480
The walls are a work of art in their own right.

498
00:37:13,160 --> 00:37:16,180
Experts combed Europe to locate the finest marble.

499
00:37:16,880 --> 00:37:22,720
In one case, an entire quarry was reserved to ensure a consistent source for the proper color and grain.

500
00:37:22,721 --> 00:37:32,424
For many years after its opening in the depths of the Depression, the Empire State Building was referred to as the Empty State Building, a wry acknowledgment of its

501
00:37:32,425 --> 00:37:34,140
embarrassing lack of tenants.

502
00:37:34,700 --> 00:37:44,929
But today, under the management of Helmsley Spear, the building has its own zip code, and the lobby directory boasts full occupancy, housing nearly 1,000 businesses

503
00:37:44,930 --> 00:37:46,800
from every continent on Earth.

504
00:37:47,800 --> 00:37:51,780
Servicing tenants requires an elaborate infrastructure of maintenance

505
00:37:51,781 --> 00:37:56,620
which begins 55 feet below street level, where concrete meets bedrock.

506
00:37:57,300 --> 00:38:02,280
Marks on the walls indicate where the ancient stream beneath the building occasionally rises,

507
00:38:02,820 --> 00:38:06,960
still waiting to irrigate John Thompson's long-forgotten farm.

508
00:38:08,460 --> 00:38:12,720
Bob Noble, chief engineer, supervises an army of workers

509
00:38:12,721 --> 00:38:15,240
who comb the building's dark labyrinths

510
00:38:15,241 --> 00:38:18,200
to constantly monitor the structure's electrical,

511
00:38:18,720 --> 00:38:21,200
heating, lighting, and phone systems.

512
00:38:22,400 --> 00:38:24,940
Maintenance of the Empire State continues

513
00:38:24,941 --> 00:38:26,600
through its towering pinnacle,

514
00:38:27,000 --> 00:38:29,600
capping the building's 102 floors.

515
00:38:30,820 --> 00:38:32,820
Where dirigibles once hoped to land

516
00:38:32,821 --> 00:38:36,660
now stands a TV transmission antenna erected in 1950,

517
00:38:36,661 --> 00:38:40,240
which by itself is as tall as a 20-story building.

518
00:38:40,241 --> 00:38:43,160
Right now we're at the top of the Empire State Building.

519
00:38:43,560 --> 00:38:46,180
This is as close to heaven as you're going to get.

520
00:38:47,060 --> 00:38:50,400
Inside the supporting tower are banks of light,

521
00:38:50,520 --> 00:38:53,800
some of them colored that bathe the building in brilliant hues

522
00:38:53,801 --> 00:38:55,840
during holidays and special events.

523
00:38:56,320 --> 00:39:00,120
But this ornate lighting also attracts some disoriented travelers.

524
00:39:00,500 --> 00:39:03,520
During the migratory season, if we had a foggy night,

525
00:39:03,700 --> 00:39:08,140
the birds would become confused with the lighting and the clouds.

526
00:39:08,141 --> 00:39:09,820
And they would actually hit into the building,

527
00:39:09,821 --> 00:39:14,900
and we would find, unfortunately, a number of birds that never made it to their destination.

528
00:39:15,460 --> 00:39:17,540
And what we started doing is, at certain times of the year,

529
00:39:17,760 --> 00:39:19,680
we knew that the birds would be migrating,

530
00:39:19,920 --> 00:39:23,940
and if we turned off the lights, except for those lights that were required for airplanes,

531
00:39:24,520 --> 00:39:25,980
that they didn't become confused.

532
00:39:26,420 --> 00:39:29,220
The interior of the spire, shielded from the public,

533
00:39:29,320 --> 00:39:32,180
is the domain of generations of nameless workers

534
00:39:32,181 --> 00:39:35,680
who have left their anonymous marks on the walls and girders.

535
00:39:36,180 --> 00:39:38,280
These are messages to the future.

536
00:39:39,820 --> 00:39:42,820
Outside the tower, nature adopts bizarre behavior.

537
00:39:43,360 --> 00:39:48,020
Exterior workers occasionally complain of being bombarded by tiny pellets

538
00:39:48,021 --> 00:39:50,600
as they're suspended high above street level.

539
00:39:51,320 --> 00:39:54,500
Scientists have discovered that the men are being struck by barley,

540
00:39:54,960 --> 00:39:57,340
blown in from windstorms in the Midwest.

541
00:39:58,120 --> 00:40:01,340
And in the winter, they report another strange phenomenon.

542
00:40:02,000 --> 00:40:05,760
Because of the updraft, it actually snows up when you're up there.

543
00:40:05,860 --> 00:40:08,340
So you see the snow kind of above you coming down,

544
00:40:08,341 --> 00:40:10,380
and then all of a sudden it's blowing back up

545
00:40:10,381 --> 00:40:12,660
and it's really unusual.

546
00:40:12,860 --> 00:40:14,480
I never saw it snow up before.

547
00:40:15,220 --> 00:40:17,740
The top of the Empire State is ringed

548
00:40:17,741 --> 00:40:19,920
with powerful microwave transmitters.

549
00:40:20,380 --> 00:40:22,780
The static interference you see in these pictures

550
00:40:22,781 --> 00:40:26,040
is the result of radiation coursing through the camera

551
00:40:26,041 --> 00:40:27,860
and, of course, through the cameraman.

552
00:40:28,740 --> 00:40:31,740
The sense of being on top of the world and seeing forever

553
00:40:31,741 --> 00:40:34,780
is what draws people to the building's observation deck

554
00:40:34,781 --> 00:40:36,400
on the 86th floor.

555
00:40:38,340 --> 00:40:41,940
Going up, going down.

556
00:40:42,700 --> 00:40:44,220
Well, it's the end of our honeymoon,

557
00:40:44,420 --> 00:40:48,000
so we thought we'd top it by going up to some of the tallest buildings in the world.

558
00:40:48,500 --> 00:40:50,520
Anywhere's romantic on your honeymoon, isn't it?

559
00:40:51,140 --> 00:40:53,060
If I'm going to be in New York City, I'm going to get the Info State Building,

560
00:40:53,220 --> 00:40:54,320
so here we are.

561
00:40:55,020 --> 00:40:56,400
Oh, man, it's a great high.

562
00:40:56,640 --> 00:40:57,460
See how high I am?

563
00:40:58,300 --> 00:41:01,820
Oh, I think we like to look down on things.

564
00:41:02,940 --> 00:41:06,460
Makes you feel slightly less insignificant, slightly less small.

565
00:41:08,340 --> 00:41:13,940
When people reach this height, they look out on a vista that extends for 80 miles,

566
00:41:14,840 --> 00:41:19,780
peering over and into New Jersey, Pennsylvania, Connecticut, Massachusetts,

567
00:41:20,140 --> 00:41:23,500
and straight down into the lush, urban jungle.

568
00:41:25,980 --> 00:41:29,120
Architecture is the means that a city has to say,

569
00:41:29,260 --> 00:41:32,480
here I am, here I stand, this is what I represent.

570
00:41:32,880 --> 00:41:37,200
The Empire State Building has become the quintessential symbol of New York,

571
00:41:37,201 --> 00:41:39,900
but not so much for architectural reasons.

572
00:41:40,180 --> 00:41:42,980
It's simply as the symbol of the might of the city.

573
00:41:43,460 --> 00:41:46,600
It is superlative in that it's the biggest of its kind.

574
00:41:47,160 --> 00:41:51,680
And everybody in New York thinks New York ought to have the biggest of whatever it may be.

575
00:41:55,120 --> 00:42:00,920
The Empire State Building is as much an experience as it is an assembly of bricks and steel.

576
00:42:01,520 --> 00:42:05,740
It brings people together for what it represents about the American will

577
00:42:05,741 --> 00:42:10,620
and holds them in its grasp with a sheer sense of awe and wonder.

578
00:42:11,000 --> 00:42:14,080
I think it's the most beautiful building in the world.

579
00:42:14,800 --> 00:42:17,140
Never mind the Taj Mahal or any of them.

580
00:42:17,660 --> 00:42:22,900
This is my lady of Fifth Avenue, the greatest lady of Fifth Avenue.

581
00:42:23,820 --> 00:42:25,240
And always will be.

